    "intro": "The Old West ran on nicknames. Henry McCarty became Billy the Kid, James Butler Hickok became Wild Bill, and Martha Jane Cannary became Calamity Jane. This **Old West name generator** uses first names common in 19th-century America, with a surname and the kind of nickname that followed people from town to town.",
    "sections": [
      {
        "heading": "Real names behind the legends",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>Billy the Kid</strong> was born Henry McCarty, and also used the name William H. Bonney.</li><li><strong>Wild Bill Hickok</strong> was James Butler Hickok.</li><li><strong>Calamity Jane</strong> was Martha Jane Cannary.</li><li><strong>Butch Cassidy</strong> was Robert LeRoy Parker; his partner, <strong>the Sundance Kid</strong>, was Harry Longabaugh, nicknamed after the jail in Sundance, Wyoming.</li><li><strong>Annie Oakley</strong>, the sharpshooter, was born Phoebe Ann Mosey.</li></ul><p>A nickname could hide a past, make a reputation or both, which is why so many outlaws had several.</p>"
      },
      {
        "heading": "Making your western name",
        "content": "<ul><li><strong>The nickname does the work.</strong> Two-Gun, Deadeye and Quickdraw make a reputation; Lefty and Dusty make a character.</li><li><strong>Plain first names fit the period:</strong> Eli, Wade and Silas; Etta, Hattie and Pearl.</li><li><strong>Give it a story.</strong> How did Lucky get the name, and is it still true?</li></ul><p>For other periods, see the <a href=\"victorian-name-generator.html\">Victorian Name Generator</a>.</p>"
      }
    ],
